nodecode-constraint-vault is a rewrite of jusst01's jusst01/constraint-vault:

  https://github.com/jusst01/constraint-vault
  commit e7949720041458c6b23821fc4091343c7b3fd0d9
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 jusst (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's promise, kept in the same words -- a small set
of exact values (a number, an address, a deadline, a hard rule) is pinned in
one JSON file under the home as the operator's own text, and it is put in
front of the model verbatim at every request, so summarising memory and
context compression cannot paraphrase, round or drop it. The three tools keep
their jobs (pin one, list them with ids, drop one by id) and keep the same
caps of fifty constraints and two thousand characters; /vault becomes
/constraints and answers list, add and drop. The store keeps each
constraint's SHA-256, and every delivery appends one line to a log beside the
store with the SHA-256 of the whole injected block, as the original did: the
delivered bytes hash to the stored ones.

What was left out, and why: the original hooked the host's pre_llm_call for
its per-turn injection and its post_compaction for a second, mid-turn
re-injection through the next tool result, with the pending session tracked in
memory. This image rebuilds its standing harness sections at every request and
re-pins them when an eviction moves the window's head, so the block rides a
standing section of its own: nothing has to detect a compaction, there is no
pending-session set, and the text is in front of the model every turn
including the first after a fold. Also left out: the VAULT_DISABLE
environment variable (a setting, inject, turns delivery off without dropping
the constraints, and "enabled": false vetoes the section), and the JSON tool
envelopes (the tools answer in sentences the model reads). A per-session log
field is kept, but sessions are named by this image, not by the plugin.
